WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.489 --> 00:00:07.799
On Monday, January 3rd, a jury found Elizabeth
Holmes guilty on four criminal fraud charges.

00:00:07.799 --> 00:00:13.330
They concluded she intentionally lied to investors
to raise heaps of money for her startup.

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:16.340
Theranos promised to revolutionize the healthcare
industry.

00:00:16.340 --> 00:00:21.769
It claimed it could detect a wide range of
diseases with just a few drops of blood.

00:00:21.769 --> 00:00:23.010
But it was all a lie.

00:00:23.010 --> 00:00:25.519
And Holmes KNEW it, according to the jury.

00:00:25.519 --> 00:00:28.470
Yet she continued to tout the technology.

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:33.700
Even convincing the likes of Oracle founder
Larry Ellison and media magnate Rupert Murdoch

00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:35.040
to invest in Theranos.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:39.970
At one point, Theranos was valued at $9 billion.

00:00:39.970 --> 00:00:45.640
This is the story of how she managed to pull
it off and how it all fell apart.

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:50.050
Before Elizabeth Holmes headed off to Stanford
to study chemical engineering, her parents

00:00:50.050 --> 00:00:55.800
gave her a copy of Marcus Aurelius’ “Meditations,”
in which the Roman emporer argued we each

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:57.190
have a purpose.

00:00:57.190 --> 00:01:02.510
For many years, she fooled people into thinking
she was indeed living a purposeful life.

00:01:02.510 --> 00:01:06.890
She dropped out of Stanford when she was 19
to build a device that she claimed could perform

00:01:06.890 --> 00:01:11.909
hundreds of medical tests - everything from
cholesterol levels to cancer -- with a few

00:01:11.909 --> 00:01:14.320
drops of blood from a finger prick.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:19.229
She sold the dream of making the world a better
place by helping people diagnose diseases

00:01:19.229 --> 00:01:21.890
WITHOUT the need to get their blood drawn.

00:01:21.890 --> 00:01:24.299
She always appealed to ethos.

00:01:24.299 --> 00:01:32.950
To me, nothing matters more than what people
go through when someone they love is found

00:01:32.950 --> 00:01:35.140
out to be really, really sick.

00:01:35.140 --> 00:01:39.990
She frequently cited the death of a beloved
uncle to skin cancer as the reason why her

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:44.680
faster, cheaper, and painless tests would
make blood testing a routine habit.

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:50.119
Who could resist such a story from a woman
who many compared to the late Apple CEO - not

00:01:50.119 --> 00:01:52.960
only because they both had a penchant for
wearing black turtlenecks.

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:58.530
To me, it’s reasonable to compare you - I
usually don’t do this - to Steve Jobs.

00:01:58.530 --> 00:02:03.189
I regard you as a visionary, next-generation
person.

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.329
Is this the kind of ridiculous pressure somebody
needs?

00:02:06.329 --> 00:02:07.900
Steve Jobs was.

00:02:07.900 --> 00:02:10.510
I don’t think there is another Steve Jobs.

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:12.360
He was a phenomenal entrepreneur.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:17.990
We’ve got an incredible opportunity to try
to uphold a legacy in Silicon Valley of changing

00:02:17.990 --> 00:02:18.990
the world.

00:02:18.990 --> 00:02:19.990
Disrupting.

00:02:19.990 --> 00:02:23.070
We’re working 24/7 to do that.

00:02:23.070 --> 00:02:26.700
Well, I understand that’s exactly how you
work.

00:02:26.700 --> 00:02:28.530
Everyone bought what she was selling.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.540
It certainly helped that Holmes had a star-studded
Board to back her up.

00:02:32.540 --> 00:02:37.710
Including former secretary of states Henry
Kissinger, George Shultz, former secretary

00:02:37.710 --> 00:02:43.380
of defense William Perry, retired Marine Corps
four-star general James Mattis, former head

00:02:43.380 --> 00:02:48.170
of the CDC William Foege, and former majority
leader of the U.S. Senate, Bill Frist.

00:02:48.170 --> 00:02:53.770
It was only a matter of time before Holmes
started receiving glowing media attention.

00:02:53.770 --> 00:02:57.480
Gracing cover after cover after cover.

00:02:57.480 --> 00:03:01.380
Forbes labeled her the youngest self-made
female billionaire.

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:04.880
Holmes did everything she could to polish
her image.

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.110
Some say she even faked a deeper voice to
better fit into the male-dominated business

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:10.110
world.

00:03:10.110 --> 00:03:15.080
In this interview from 2005, we hear Holmes
using a higher-pitched voice before appearing

00:03:15.080 --> 00:03:25.580
to switch to a lower one.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:30.110
Holmes checked off all the boxes to become
the darling of Silicon Valley.

00:03:30.110 --> 00:03:32.290
She dropped out of a prestigious school.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.290
She was young.

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:35.710
And secretive about her innovation.

00:03:35.710 --> 00:03:40.140
In the world of tech, a certain mystique was
the norm, just like how Apple operated under

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:41.160
jobs.

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:45.690
She took investors’ money on the condition
that she wouldn’t have to reveal how Theranos’

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:47.250
technology worked.

00:03:47.250 --> 00:03:51.760
No peer-reviewed scientific articles existed
to back up its claims.

00:03:51.760 --> 00:03:54.380
Not everyone was so easily fooled, however.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:58.960
John Carreyrou who was then a reporter at
the Wall Street Journal, exposed Theranos

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:04.690
for what it was in a series of articles in
2015 and later a best-selling book.

00:04:04.690 --> 00:04:09.240
He reported that Theranos’ blood-testing
machine couldn't give accurate results, so

00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:14.090
Theranos secretly ran its samples through
machines used by traditional blood-testing

00:04:14.090 --> 00:04:15.330
companies.

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:19.849
Everything Holmes did was strategic, down
to the name of her company’s testing device.

00:04:19.849 --> 00:04:25.910
Holmes named her machine Edison after Thomas
Edison, who famously said: "I have not failed.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:29.080
I've just found 10,000 ways that won't work."

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:33.740
Holmes referenced Edison’s quote during
a commencement address at Pepperdine University

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:38.270
when she said: "We code-named our product
the Edison because we assumed we’d have

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:42.789
to fail 10,000 times to get it to work the
ten-thousand-and-first.

00:04:42.789 --> 00:04:43.819
And we did."

00:04:43.819 --> 00:04:46.340
(2015)
Except, they didn’t.

00:04:46.340 --> 00:04:50.840
Walgreens reportedly brought Theranos tech
into its pharmacies without even testing the

00:04:50.840 --> 00:04:52.110
technology.

00:04:52.110 --> 00:04:57.169
The first patient to testify against Elizabeth
Holmes was Brittany Gould, who took a Theranos

00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:00.699
test at a Walgreens, which revealed she was
miscarrying.

00:05:00.699 --> 00:05:03.189
It would have been her fourth miscarriage.

00:05:03.189 --> 00:05:08.729
To be safe, she decided to take tests at a
different clinic that confirmed she was, in

00:05:08.729 --> 00:05:10.090
fact, still pregnant.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:13.719
She eventually gave birth to a healthy baby
girl.

00:05:13.719 --> 00:05:17.219
During the trial, Holmes’ lawyers argued
that it wasn’t she who was deceiving the

00:05:17.219 --> 00:05:20.349
public but that she was herself deceived.

00:05:20.349 --> 00:05:25.349
She claimed Theranos’ COO Sunny Balwani,
whom she had been dating, had manipulated

00:05:25.349 --> 00:05:26.349
her.

00:05:26.349 --> 00:05:28.810
But prosecutors weren’t buying it.

00:05:28.810 --> 00:05:33.120
Prosecutor Jeff Schenk said in his closing
arguments: “She chose to be dishonest with

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:35.139
investors and patients.

00:05:35.139 --> 00:05:38.570
That choice was not only callous, it was criminal.”

00:05:38.570 --> 00:05:43.560
The jury agreed, finding her guilty on four
of 11 charges of fraud.

00:05:43.560 --> 00:05:47.180
She faces up to 20 years in prison.

